{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the days of Adam after he begot Shet</b> — Because of the extreme longevity of these early generations, Scripture specifies their years both before they begot children and afterward, and then gives a total sum at the end, through the generations after the Flood. The reason for their long lives is that the first Adam, the handiwork of HaKadosh Barukh Hu, was created in the perfection of strength and stature, and even after the decree of mortality was imposed upon him, it was in his nature to live a very long time. And when the Flood came upon the earth, the atmosphere became corrupted for them, and their days kept diminishing, for until the Flood their days were at that great length, and some of them lived even longer than Adam. And Shem, who was born before the Flood, lived six hundred years — the strong constitution with which he was born benefited him, while the corrupted atmosphere harmed him. And his sons born after the Flood had their days shortened and they reverted to four hundred. And you can see that this persisted among them until the Dispersion (פלגה), and when the change of atmospheres prevailed over them at the Dispersion, their days were shortened yet again, for Peleg, in whose days the earth was divided, reverted to half their years, two hundred years. And it appears that in the generations of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, the years of the people were seventy or eighty years, as Moshe our Master mentioned in his prayer (Psalms 90:10). But the righteous in their generations — the fear of HaShem adds days to them, for Pharaoh marveled at Jacob and considered his lifespan extraordinary compared to the days of his fathers, as he said, 'and they did not attain the days of the years of the life of my fathers in the days of their sojourning' (Genesis 47:9). And the statement of the Rav in the Guide for the Perplexed (II:47) does not seem right to me — that the length of those years was only in these specific individuals mentioned, while the rest of the people in those generations had the normal, customary years of life as is usual. And he said that this exceptional longevity in each particular man was due to his regimen and diet, or by way of miracle. Behold, these are words of wind, for why should this miracle happen to them — and they are neither prophets nor righteous men deserving of a miracle done for them — generation after generation? And how could proper regimen and diet extend their lives many times over beyond everyone in that generation? And it is possible that others too followed that good regimen, and it would have prolonged life for all of them or for most of them, so how could the knowledge of that good regimen not reach even one of all the sons of Noah after the Flood? Rather, they retained some of the wisdom of their forefathers, and it kept diminishing from generation to generation."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The Rav is Maimonides; the reference is to Guide for the Perplexed II:47. The phrase 'words of wind' (דברי רוח) is Ramban's standard dismissive formulation for arguments he rejects."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:25:14.726083251Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}